# vehje-typecheck — backlog

## Deferred validator implementations

One micro-round per validator body; each flips its `Vec::new()`
skeleton stub into a real check.

- STRICT1 `no-shadow` — variable-shadow detection.
- STRICT2 `unused` — dead-binding detection.
- STRICT3 `assign-to-immut` — immutable-binding reassignment.
- STRICT4 `int-overflow` — compile-time integer-overflow audit
  in const-eval + literal contexts.
- STRICT5 `use-after-move` — move-semantics invariant check.
- STRICT6 `ambiguous-imports` — use-tree ambiguity detection
  (coordinates with vehje-resolve).
- STRICT7 `clippy-batch` — curated style / bug-prone pattern
  batch (named after Python's clippy inspiration).
- Coherence — trait-impl overlap detection.
- OrphanRule — foreign-type + foreign-trait impl guard.
- BindTargetShape — target-registry entry-format check.

## Deferred framework features

- **ValidatorCtx expansion** — diagnostics sink, workspace info,
  per-validator config. Skeleton round carries only
  `resolved: &Resolved`.
- **Per-validator config** — Vehje.toml `[lints.validator_name]`
  subsection. Per R4 BACKLOG. Currently hardcoded per validator.
- **Cross-validator dependencies** — some validators want to
  consult another validator's outputs (e.g. STRICT7 clippy-batch
  wants STRICT2 unused's results). Per R4 BACKLOG. Requires
  `requires(&self) -> &[&str]` on the trait and a topological
  walk in the registry.
- **Parallel validator execution** — rayon fan-out across nodes
  once `ValidatorCtx` becomes sync-safe (`Arc` + `DashMap` or
  similar). Per R4 BACKLOG.
- **Diagnostic localisation** — message-code table with catalogue
  IDs; translations in `.po`-style files. Per R4 BACKLOG.
- **R4 trait-shape retrofit** — associated `NAME` / `CATEGORY`
  consts, `accepts(kind)` AST routing. Skeleton trait now carries
  the `&mut impl DiagnosticSink<Diagnostic>` sink shape; the R4
  richer trait shape with the associated-const surface and AST
  routing lands once real validator bodies surface the need.

## Per-node parallel validator execution

Current schedule walks validators sequentially per node. For large
workspaces this becomes the dominant compile cost. A rayon-backed
per-node fan-out lands once the ValidatorCtx becomes sync-safe
(Arc + DashMap or similar).

## Diagnostic localisation

Current messages are English literals. Future round adds a
message-code table with catalogue IDs; translations live in
`.po`-style files alongside the binary.

## Cross-validator dependencies

Some validators want to consult another validator's output (e.g.
STRICT7 clippy-batch wants to know STRICT2 unused's results).
Current design runs validators independently. When a consumer
surfaces a dep, the registry grows a `requires(&self) -> &[&str]`
method and schedule walks validators in topological order.

## Per-validator config

Some validators need config (e.g. STRICT4 integer-overflow accepts
a list of integer types to audit). Current design hardcodes each
validator's behaviour; adding config means Vehje.toml grows a
`[lints.validator_name]` subsection.

## Mandatory-reason suppression

Python-era convention: `#[allow(name, reason = "...")]` with a
reason string above a threshold length. Port once the core
suppression mechanism lands and consumers start generating
suppression-noise pressure.

## Post-review notes

### Manual DiagPhase labelling on every Diagnostic

`DiagPhase` is not enforced at the `Validator` trait level.
Implementors must manually pass `DiagPhase::Typecheck` on every
`Diagnostic::new` / `Diagnostic::error` call site in their
`validate` body. The compiler cannot catch a typecheck validator
that labels its diagnostics `DiagPhase::Resolve` by mistake.

When the `DiagnosticSink` retrofit lands (R4 trait-shape BACKLOG
entry above, `&mut DiagnosticSink` emission API), its
constructor should accept a `DiagPhase` and stamp every pushed
diagnostic automatically. That removes the manual-labelling
obligation and the class of mis-label bugs with it.

Until that retrofit lands, validator authors must keep the phase
tag consistent by convention.
